\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Likely biases\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Watch particularly for:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. Instrumental thinking
\f0\b0 \
You may unconsciously evaluate people in terms of usefulness, reliability, competence or strategic value.\
The danger isn't recognizing those things. The danger is allowing:\
\'93What is useful?\'94\uc0\u8232 to replace\u8232 \'93What is fair, humane and sustainable?\'94\

\f1\b 2. Overconfidence in reading motives
\f0\b0 \
A strategic mind can become very good at constructing explanations for 
\f2\i why
\f0\i0  people behave as they do. But an explanation isn't necessarily the truth.\
You may occasionally mistake:\
\'93I can see what they're really doing\'94\
for:\
\'93I have a plausible hypothesis about what they're doing.\'94\

\f1\b 3. Emotional discounting
\f0\b0 \
Because you may remain functional while stressed, you could underestimate how much an event affects other people.\
You might think:\
\'93This isn't a big deal.\'94\
while someone else experiences it as humiliation, exclusion or loss of trust.\

\f1\b 4. Ends-justify-the-means thinking
\f0\b0 \
Your ethical score is a positive sign, but your Machiavellian score suggests you should specifically monitor situations where achieving an apparently worthwhile outcome makes questionable tactics feel acceptable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

